INT. RUINED OPERA HOUSE - FIELD HOSPITAL - NIGHT

The stage is a ward. Cots under gilded boxes. Candles in wine bottles. Above, a painted ceiling of cherubs, half of it fallen onto the stalls.

SISTER AGNIESZKA, 30s, moves between the cots with a basin and a rag. Her habit is gray with dust. She stops at the last bed.

SERGEANT HOLLIS BRANTLEY, 20s, lies on the final cot. His left arm is bandaged to the shoulder. He has been awake for hours.

She sets the basin down. He watches her wring the rag.

BRANTLEY
Is it quiet out there?

AGNIESZKA
It has been since midnight.

He nods. She presses the cloth to his forehead, and he flinches, then lets her.

BRANTLEY
You have a kind hand, Sister.

AGNIESZKA
I have a tired hand.

She smiles slightly. He almost does. The candle flames lean in a draft and straighten.

BRANTLEY
I haven't heard a bomb in four hours.

AGNIESZKA
Don't say it aloud.

BRANTLEY
Why?

AGNIESZKA
Because the building listens.

He laughs, then winces. She adjusts the blanket. Her fingers brush the dog tags at his throat. She holds them a moment, turned to the candle.

Her face does not change. Her hand does not move.

AGNIESZKA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Brantley.

BRANTLEY
Yes?

AGNIESZKA
That is an English name.

BRANTLEY
It's my name.

She lets go of the tags. Straightens. Takes up the basin.

AGNIESZKA
I will bring you water.

BRANTLEY
Sister, I'm in the right ward, aren't I?

She stops. She does not turn.

AGNIESZKA
It is the only ward there is.

She walks away, careful not to hurry. At the proscenium she sets down the basin, steps behind the curtain, and presses her back to the wall.

Her hand goes inside her habit. It comes out holding a pistol.
